es should release the  records on thursday the appeals court  asked lawyers for trump congress and the  biden administration to submit briefs  ahead of oral arguments now set for  november 30th  dina and terry let's break this down  dina i'm going to start with you former  president trump is working overtime to  keep these records a secret  what information could possibly be in  those records and how could they  possibly incriminate donald trump  the documents that are under this  executive privilege claim include  presidential diaries  activity logs  call logs visitor logs and draft  speeches so all of those could be  helpful to the committee to determine  who president trump was talking to  around january 6 what kind of phone  calls he did or did not make that day in  the middle of the attack on the capitol  and also these draft speeches what was  the language like  you know in the drafts before this  speech he did ultimately give that day  yeah and terry president biden has  stated that former president trump  cannot assert executive privilege and  that trump must turn over these records  could that hurt biden in the future  it could hurt biden but i think that is  the proper argument i think that this  privilege is an executive privilege and  it only applies to the incumbent because  he is the executive at that time but  eventually biden will be out of office  and it could very well be one day people  are calling for his diaries and his  calendars and he would lose the argument  at that point if in fact it prevails  yeah it's a temporary injunction and we  will see how it plays out in court and  when we come back the contentious case  of accused kenosha shooter kyle  rittenhouse is nearing verdict watch  hear from the case's judge who explains  the potential outcomes written house  could face  welcome back to law on crime daily and  both sides rest in the fast-paced trial  of wisconsin versus kyle rittenhouse  ahead of monday's jury instructions both  sides focused on the charges in the case  eighteen-year-old rittenhouse faces  charges of reckless murder and attempted  murder in connection to the august 25th  2020 shootings in kenosha wisconsin  two men joseph rosenbaum and anthony  hubert died as a result of the shootings  gage gross kroitz was shot and wounded  but survived britton house's team argues  the defendant was acting in self-defense  that night the judge ruled the jury  could stay home on friday as the defense  and prosecution went head on written  house's lesser included chargers in the  end judge schroeder explained to  rittenhouse what outcome the jury could  find after hearing closing arguments on  monday  by  having a lesser included offense  included  you're  raising the risk of conviction  although you are  you're  there's you're avoiding the possibility  that the jury  will  end up compromising on the more serious  crime  and you're also  increasing the up  decreasing the risk that you'll end up  with a second trial because the jury is  unable to agree  if the jury finds that  none of the crimes charged in the  sequence  charged or submitted  has been proved beyond a reasonable  doubt then they must  quit you they must find you not guilty  and they cannot convict you of more than  one crime  on each count  okay so a less dramatic day in court but  an important one nonetheless  dina now that the case is drawing to a  close how well do you think rittenhouse  has been able to establish a  self-defense argument  what we all saw in the courtroom their  his testimony the witnesses the defense  put forward was very strong for the  defense but we cannot discount what  happened today as you said it's less  dramatic but very helpful for the  prosecution because they're going to  have an instruction regarding  provocation did he provoke it  what should he have retreated when you  see the facts of this case that's  essentially what happened he did provoke  it and he didn't retreat and that might  be the difference